<ToolPage
  kind="guide"
  title="The Real Math of Picking Up a Fourth 12 (Nursing Edition)"
  description="Why the fourth 12-hour shift is the best-paid and worst-priced shift of a nurse's week — FLSA overtime on a differential-raised rate, incentive stacking, taxes, and the recovery cost."
  h1="The Real Math of the Fourth 12"
>
  <p>
    Every nurse on 3×12s knows the text message: <em>"Critical staffing Saturday — pickup bonus
    approved."</em> Whether to say yes is a personal call, but the money side has real math, and
    most people do it wrong in both directions — underestimating the pay and underestimating the
    cost. Here is the honest version.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the fourth shift pays disproportionately</h2>
  <p>
    Three 12s is 36 hours — under the federal 40-hour rule, your scheduled week contains zero
    overtime. The fourth 12 changes the arithmetic: hours 37–40 pay straight time, but
    <strong>hours 41–48 pay overtime</strong>, and the overtime rate is 1.5× your
    <em>regular rate</em> — which includes your night and weekend differentials, not just base pay.
    A $42 base with a $5 night differential produces a regular rate well above $42, and an OT rate
    well above $63. Stack a pickup incentive on top and the fourth shift can gross 40–60% more than
    a regular one. Run your exact numbers in the
    <a href="/nurse-shift-pay-calculator">nurse shift pay calculator</a> — the OT interaction is
    exactly what it models.
  </p>

  <h2>The tax illusion</h2>
  <p>
    The classic pickup complaint — "overtime is taxed to death" — is a withholding illusion. A big
    check triggers higher <em>withholding</em> because payroll annualizes that single period, but
    your actual tax on the extra dollars is just your marginal rate; the difference reconciles at
    refund time. Overtime hours are worth what the regular-rate math says, not what the shrunken
    first check implies.
  </p>

  <h2>California and daily-overtime states</h2>
  <p>
    Federal law counts weekly hours, but California pays overtime after 8 hours in a day and double
    time after 12 — so a 12.5-hour California shift already contains premium time before any
    pickup, and the fourth-shift calculus shifts accordingly. A handful of other states have daily
    rules; know yours before pricing a pickup, because the same shift can differ by hundreds of
    dollars across a state line.
  </p>

  <h2>The cost side nobody invoices</h2>
  <p>
    A fourth 12 consumes more than 12 hours: commute, wind-down, and the recovery sleep that
    bleeds into your first off-day. On a week where your three shifts were nights, the fourth
    effectively spends one of your family days — price it that way. The sustainable pattern most
    veteran nurses converge on is selective pickup: yes when the shift extends an existing block
    (fatigue already paid for), no when it splits a recovery window. Your
    <a href="/2-2-3-panama-schedule-calculator">rotation calendar</a> shows exactly which pickups
    extend blocks and which shatter breaks — look before you reply to the text.
  </p>

  <h2>Making pickups a strategy instead of a reflex</h2>
  <p>
    If the money is the point, aim pickups at differential-heavy slots — weekend nights multiply
    base, differential, and OT together — and track which
    <a href="/biweekly-payday-calculator">payday</a> each pickup lands on so the "bonus month"
    checks are deliberate. If the money is not the point, protect the anchor of your
    <a href="/night-shift-sleep-calculator">sleep plan</a> first; no incentive bonus prices what
    chronic under-sleep costs a nursing career. Either way, decide with the calculator, not the
    staffing office's urgency.
  </p>
